# Third-party code

Labrador is MIT licensed (see LICENSE). This file records what in the working
tree is not Labrador's to license.

The sections on content that is deliberately not distributed — the audio and
the compiled fonts — went with `game/` to the paint-shooter's own repository,
because that is where the content they describe lives. This repository ships
no content beyond `samples/`.

## Code carried in this repository

### Microsoft — DirectX Tool Kit samples (MIT)

Three things were adopted from Microsoft's DirectX samples and kept rather than
rewritten — four files, `device_resources` being a header and a source. Two of
them carry their original file headers; the third never had one, being a
fragment of a sample's own header rather than a file in its own right:

- `engine/render/d3d11/device_resources.{h,cpp}` — "DeviceResources.h - A
  wrapper for the Direct3D 11 device and swapchain"
- `engine/core/step_timer.h` — "StepTimer.h - A simple timer that provides
  elapsed time information"
- `engine/render/throw_if_failed.h` — `com_exception` and `ThrowIfFailed`,
  which DirectXTK's samples carry inside their own headers. It is a separate
  file here because `device_resources` is not its only caller — and since a
  second Direct3D backend landed, not even its only folder of callers, which
  is what decided where it lives.

Copyright (c) Microsoft Corporation. Licensed under the MIT License.
Source: https://github.com/microsoft/DirectXTK

All three have been modified, and the modifications are listed rather than
summarised, because "recognisably Microsoft's" is only checkable if the
departures from upstream are written down:

- All three were brought into the `labrador` namespace, and two of them have
  moved since: `device_resources` into the D3D11 backend folder when the
  renderer seam was cut, and `throw_if_failed.h` out of `engine/core/` and into
  `engine/render/`, because the one module everything may lean on was carrying
  `<Windows.h>` and an `HRESULT` for the sole benefit of the two Direct3D
  backends. It is in neither backend's folder because both call it and a
  backend's headers are its own; the file itself carries that argument.
- `device_resources.h` renames upstream's `IDeviceNotify` to
  `D3DDeviceNotify`, because `DeviceNotify` is now the engine seam's name in
  `engine/render/renderer.h` and is what a game implements.
- `device_resources.cpp` includes this repository's `throw_if_failed.h` in
  place of the sample's own copy.
- The pool of deferred contexts was engine code, not Microsoft's, and has
  been removed: each of the renderer's views owns the context it records
  into, which is where that lifetime always belonged.

They remain recognisably Microsoft's, with the last engine-authored code out
of them.

**`engine/render/d3d12/device_resources.{h,cpp}` is not one of them**, and the
shared filename is the reason to say so here rather than only in the file. It
is Labrador's own, written for the Direct3D 12 backend and following this
repository's conventions rather than the sample's - snake_case methods,
trailing-underscore members, no `m_` - which is the visible difference between
the two files if you open them side by side. It solves the same problem for a
different API and borrows no code from the D3D12 flavour of Microsoft's sample;
what it does keep is the name `D3DDeviceNotify`, so that the two backends spell
the same idea the same way.

**`engine/render/vulkan/device_resources.{h,cpp}` is not one of them either**,
and it shares that filename with two files rather than one now, which is a
further reason to say so. It is Labrador's own, written for the Vulkan backend
and following this repository's conventions; it borrows no code from LunarG's
or Khronos' samples. It keeps the shape of the D3D12 file next door on purpose
- a timeline semaphore is an `ID3D12Fence` spelt differently - and the naming it
keeps from that one is `VulkanDeviceNotify` beside `D3DDeviceNotify`, so that
three backends spell the same idea the same way.

### Christer Ericson — Real-Time Collision Detection

`engine/math/ericson_math.{h,cpp}` transcribes closest-point and intersection
routines from *Real-Time Collision Detection* (Christer Ericson, Morgan
Kaufmann, 2005), adapted from the book's 3D forms to this engine's 2D types.
The file names the book at the top.

The code in that book is provided by the author for use in readers' own
software. It is transcribed and adapted here rather than copied wholesale, and
is attributed for that reason rather than because a licence compels it. If you
are redistributing this engine commercially, read the book's own terms.

